# Changelog

All notable changes to Daftari are documented here.
Format: [Semantic Versioning](https://semver.org)

---

## [6.16.0] — 2026-09-26 — a paid vendor finally receives the plan they paid for

v6.15.0 documented the payment path end to end and listed four defects behind it.
This release fixes all four. One of them meant Daftari could take a vendor's money,
verify the receipt as correct, and leave them on the free tier — the single most
expensive thing in the codebase, and it had been shipping quietly the whole time.

### Fixed

- **A verified payment now grants the plan.** `daftari_subscriptions` was written in
  exactly one place, the signup trigger. Both settlement paths stopped at
  `status = 'verified'` and never touched entitlement, and RLS denies every client
  write, so nothing else could do it either. Activation now happens on the
  `pending -> verified` transition itself, so the C2B path, the admin path, and any
  future settlement path are all covered by one rule rather than by remembering to
  edit each function. The trigger shares the verifying transaction, so a failure
  rolls the verification back instead of recording a payment nobody received.
  Verified against production: 33 assertions driving the real vendor, admin, and
  C2B paths, including that a rejected claim grants nothing and a client still
  cannot self-upgrade.
- **Our faults are no longer reported as the payer's.** A dropped connection, an
  expired session, an unprovisioned business, or a request we built wrong all
  rendered "The payer didn't complete the payment". Each now says what actually
  happened, an offline device is refused before the push instead of after, and the
  generic fallback no longer names the payer at all. Where we genuinely cannot know
  whether the push landed, the copy says so.
- **A possibly-lost payment can no longer read as an all-clear.** The device gives
  up at 5 minutes; the operator counter flagged at 30. It was also the only money
  counter excluded from the attention check and drawn in reassuring amber. It now
  fires at 5 minutes, raises an alarm, and renders red. The copy no longer claims
  "Nothing was charged" — from a missing callback that is not knowable.
- **`mpesa_code` no longer holds a payer's phone number.** Two SMS patterns returned
  the sender's phone as the receipt code, and that single value was written to both
  the transaction's code column and the customer's phone field. Code and phone are
  separate fields now. Checked production: no rows were affected, so no repair was
  needed.

### Verification notes

Three gates in this area were decorative and are now real, each confirmed by
reintroducing the defect and watching the gate fail: the ReconciliationScreen test
hand-copied the attention logic into its mock and so could not see any real change;
the SMS test asserted that a phone number *was* the transaction code; and a
refusal assertion in the new activation script was passing on an HTTP 401 that meant
the request was never authenticated at all.

Full suite: 725 tests across 65 files, plus 51 Deno edge-function tests, typecheck,
lint, i18n parity, and build.

---

## [6.15.0] — 2026-09-26 — support, status, satisfaction, and an honest payment ledger

v6.14.4 made the payment machinery correct. This release makes the product
*legible*: a vendor can find a human, an admin can tell everyone something, a
vendor can tell us whether we helped, and the legal pages finally say what
happens to their money — in Kiswahili.

It also closes a documentation debt that turned out to be a safety debt. Reading
the payment path end to end surfaced four defects that no test was watching.

### Added

- **Status banner.** An admin can post a notice ("maintenance tonight", "M-Pesa
  is fixed") that every user sees on next open, dismissable, with no release.
  Writing goes through `publish_status_message()` / `retire_status_message()`,
  which are `is_admin()`-gated security-definer functions. There is deliberately
  no client INSERT policy: an RLS policy for authenticated users would let any
  signed-in vendor post a banner to every other vendor. Verified in production —
  a vendor reads a banner but gets `NOT_ADMIN` publishing or retiring one, and
  retiring removes it for everyone on the next fetch with no cache purge.
- **Satisfaction survey.** One question, 7 days after a vendor's *first sale*
  (not 7 days after install), as a card under the profit hero. Never a modal.
  "Already answered" is a real query against `daftari_feedback`, so answering on
  an old phone does not re-ask on a new one, and a failed query resolves to "do
  not show". A failed write does **not** show "thank you".
- **`docs/vendor-payment-flow.md`.** The two M-Pesa flows — what a vendor
  collects, and what a vendor pays — documented separately, with 32
  machine-verified `file:line` citations and the known gaps written down.
- **`scripts/test-stk-sandbox.sh`.** A gate that exits non-zero unless Safaricom
  returns a `CheckoutRequestID`, and treats HTTP 200 as *not* success because
  the function returns 200 with `result: "FAILED"` on an upstream refusal.
- **`scripts/daraja-diagnostics.sh`.** Resolved Daraja wiring read from inside
  the edge function, because `DARAJA_ENV` and `DARAJA_MERCHANT_KIND` are
  write-only secrets and cannot be inspected from the shell at all. Reports
  routing and credential *presence/length*; never a key, secret or passkey value.
- **Legal pages in Kiswahili**, plus new **M-Pesa Payments** and **Subscription &
  Billing** sections. Prices are interpolated from `PRICING` at render time, so
  the legal page cannot quote a price the checkout no longer charges.

### Changed

- `SUPPORT.EMAIL` is now `support.daftari.app@gmail.com` everywhere, and Settings
  gained **Contact Support** and **App Version** rows. The footer version line
  remains as branding.
- The Legal header follows the active tab. It was hardcoded to the privacy title,
  so the terms of service were displayed under the heading "Privacy Policy".
- The policy date is labelled "Policy last reviewed" instead of borrowing the
  reconciliation label, which rendered as "Verified: <date>" above a document
  that is explicitly a draft. Every legal section now carries an unmissable
  draft-pending-lawyers notice.
- The STK callback's documented guarantees are enumerated with the test that
  proves each one, and the limit is stated plainly: STK does not re-query
  Safaricom, so a lost callback leaves a `PENDING` request for a human. The
  guarantee is not eventual settlement.

### Fixed

- `scripts/run-stk-drill.sh` no longer carries a duplicated, broken API-key
  parser; it sources `scripts/_supabase-keys.sh` and now reaches Safaricom.
- The STK failure path is diagnosable. Unrecognised upstream error bodies are
  preserved (redacted and truncated) instead of being flattened to
  `DAROJA_ERROR`, which is how a wrong-product push was indistinguishable from a
  wrong-environment push.

### Known gaps (documented, not fixed)

- **A verified subscription payment does not activate the plan.** The only write
  to `daftari_subscriptions` is the signup trigger; neither
  `settle_subscription_c2b` nor `admin_resolve_subscription` touches it. An
  operator must update the row by hand. This is the most serious finding in this
  release and it is a money gap, not a cosmetic one.
- An offline STK push reports *"The payer didn't complete the payment"*, because
  every non-specific error code collapses to `stk_failed`. Our fault is displayed
  as the customer's.
- The device gives up on a push at 5 minutes; `stale_pending` flags at 30.
- The SMS parser stores the sender's **phone number** in the field named
  `mpesa_code` for the two "received from" patterns.

### Verification

692 Vitest tests, 10 Deno edge-function tests, typecheck, lint, and i18n parity
all pass. The STK callback e2e suite passes. The live Daraja sandbox gate
(`scripts/test-stk-sandbox.sh`) **fails by design**: the sandbox passkey is 89
characters where Daraja issues 32 or 64, so Safaricom refuses every push with
`400.002.02 Invalid TransactionType`. Re-copy the passkey and the business
shortcode from the same Daraja product page — see `docs/runbook/daraja-stk-push.md`.

## [6.14.4] — 2026-09-26 — C2B: a customer who pays by hand now settles without a human

v6.14.3 wired collection to the real Till and gave hand-paid upgrades an
audited closing path — which an operator still had to perform by eye. The
underlying reason is architectural, and worth stating plainly: **Daraja calls
us back only for pushes we initiate.** A customer who types the Till into their
own M-PESA app generates no callback at all, so Daftari never learns the money
arrived.

C2B is the product that fixes it. We register a ConfirmationURL and Safaricom
POSTs every payment into our shortcode.

### Added

- `parseC2bConfirmation`, `buildC2bVerdict`, `buildC2bRegisterRequest` in the
  shared protocol module, so the same pure code runs in the edge function and
  the test suite.
- `c2b-confirmation` edge function: parse the claim, record it, verify it with
  Safaricom, then settle.
- `daftari_c2b_notifications` audit table — one row per notification Safaricom
  sent, with the verification outcome and raw payload. No client policy: it holds
  payer phone numbers.
- `settle_subscription_c2b()`, which re-checks the confirmed audit row in the
  database before it will move anything.
- `scripts/register-c2b-urls.sh` for one-time URL registration.

### The security property, and why it is shaped this way

The ConfirmationURL is an unauthenticated public endpoint, so its body is
attacker-controlled. A payload is recorded as a **claim**, never as proof of
payment. Settlement requires an independent round trip to Safaricom's
Transaction Status Query API to confirm the TransID. If that check cannot be
performed — no credentials, Daraja down, network error — the notification stays
`UNVERIFIED`, the upgrade stays pending, and a human resolves it later.

Verified against production, not merely asserted:

| Attempt | Result |
| --- | --- |
| Stranger POSTs a fabricated confirmation | `200` (so Safaricom never retries), recorded `safaricom_confirmed=false`, `UNVERIFIED` |
| Authenticated client inserts a fake audit row | denied by RLS |
| Anonymous caller invokes the settlement RPC | `permission denied` |
| Service role forces a settlement | `NOT_CONFIRMED: ... was never confirmed with Safaricom` |

Matching is deliberately strict: an exact receipt beats a bare amount match, and
two identical-amount pending requests with no receipt is **refused rather than
guessed**, because guessing settles the wrong customer. Retries are idempotent
through a unique `trans_id`, since Safaricom re-POSTs anything it believes went
unacknowledged.

### Fixed

`c2b-confirmation` would have shipped JWT-gated by default, because that is the
Supabase CLI default. Safaricom sends no JWT, so every customer-initiated
payment would have been rejected at the gateway — the v6.13.0 outage recurring
on a new endpoint, and silently, because a rejected callback still looks clean
to a health view. The deploy script now deploys it `--no-verify-jwt` and
**asserts** that posture, while still proving `stk-push` and
`send-daily-close-push` remain authenticated.

### Security

A Daraja Postman collection was found in `public/` and deployed. `public/` is
static-asset territory, so it was publicly downloadable. It turned out to carry
no real credentials — basic-auth username empty, password absent, bearer value a
placeholder — but it did map our API surface, and it should never have been
served. It is now gitignored under `.secrets-local/` at mode 600, and
`npm run build` runs `scripts/guard-public-secrets.sh` first, which fails the
build on a filename or content match for consumer key/secret, passkey, password
or basic/bearer auth.

### Added (operator)

- **Admin provisioned.** `hellodannymuhoro@gmail.com` promoted via
  `scripts/bootstrap-admin.sh`, so the CEO dashboard and payment queue are
  reachable. Promotion no longer requires handing the account password to a
  script; it verifies the row and tells you to confirm in-app, and only performs
  the end-to-end `is_admin()` check when `ADMIN_PASSWORD` is set.

### Still outstanding

- **`DARAJA_PASSKEY` is still unset and the STK drill has still never run.** The
  Postman collection is Safaricom's public demo export and does not contain the
  per-app consumer key, secret or passkey; those exist only on the product page
  in the Daraja portal. Nothing in this release is proven against Safaricom.
- **C2B is registered nowhere yet.** The code, migration, function and audit
  trail are live, but `register-c2b-urls.sh` has not been run and a C2B
  shortcode has not been confirmed. Note that a Till number is generally **not** a
  C2B shortcode — Safaricom issues a Paybill for C2B.
- No alerting, `support@daftari.app` still unverified, legal text still
  unreviewed and English-only against a Kiswahili default.

## [6.14.3] — 2026-09-26 — The Till is real, and a hand-paid Till payment can finally be settled

The pay instructions told customers to send money to a Till that the code had
hardcoded the wrong routing for, and the "I Have Paid" button wrote a row that
nothing could ever close. Both are now wired to the same Till, and the manual
path has an audited closing mechanism.

### Fixed

`stk-push` sent `TransactionType: CustomerPayBillOnline` and used the store
number for both `BusinessShortCode` and `PartyB`, for a **Till** merchant. The
payload was well-formed and wrong, so Safaricom would have refused it. Merchant
kind is now explicit and resolved per kind:

- `till` → `BusinessShortCode` = store number, `PartyB` = till number,
  `CustomerBuyGoodsOnline`
- `paybill` → `BusinessShortCode` = `PartyB` = paybill number,
  `CustomerPayBillOnline`

The STK password is derived from the same `BusinessShortCode` the payload
carries, which is what Safaricom checks. Unknown merchant kinds and missing
routing secrets now throw at the config boundary and surface as
`503 DARAJA_NOT_CONFIGURED` rather than a bad request going upstream.

### Added

A hand-paid Till payment produces **no** Daraja callback — Daraja only calls
back for pushes we initiate — so Daftari cannot learn about it on its own. The
M-Pesa receipt code is the only unique handle, and nothing was recording it.

- `mpesa_receipt`, `resolved_at`, `resolved_by` on subscription requests, with
  a database-level guarantee that a request cannot be marked verified without a
  receipt, and that one receipt cannot settle two requests.
- `admin_subscription_queue()` and `admin_resolve_subscription()`,
  `security definer` + `is_admin()`, so a non-admin or a missing gate yields no
  access rather than partial access.
- An operator worklist in the admin dashboard, oldest pending first, with the
  payer, amount, wait time, and Confirm/Reject. A refused approval keeps the row
  and shows the server's reason, because the money is still unmatched.
- Receipt capture in the upgrade sheet, so the code the customer already has is
  recorded up front instead of being asked for later.
- `scripts/bootstrap-admin.sh` to promote an existing account, and
  `scripts/verify-manual-reconciliation.sh` (17 checks, negative and positive
  path) as the evidence.

### Fixed (harness)

`scripts/run-stk-drill.sh` could never have produced a prompt, and would have
failed looking like a payment fault:

- it created a user but no business, then posted an all-zero `businessId`, so
  every run stopped at `403 BUSINESS_MISMATCH`;
- it wrote `owner_id` and passed the business UUID, but ownership is `user_id`
  and the gate matches on `local_id` — verified by calling the live function,
  which now clears ownership and stops only at the missing passkey;
- cleanup left the income row behind, quietly corrupting revenue.

`scripts/daraja-preflight.sh` also required only the store number for a till,
reporting READY for a merchant that would then be charged with the store number
as `PartyB`. Both routing values are now required.

The runbook's environment section still documented the long-removed
`DARAJA_SHORTCODE` and omitted the till/paybill routing difference; it now
carries the table above and states that sandbox credentials are not live ones.

### Not done — deliberately, and still blocking launch

- **No real STK push has completed.** `DARAJA_PASSKEY` is not in the project
  and the exact `BusinessShortCode` for the Daraja product is unconfirmed, so
  nothing here has been proven against Safaricom. Only the passkey is missing to
  run the drill.
- **No admin is provisioned.** Production has zero admins, so the queue and the
  pre-existing dashboard are unreachable. `bootstrap-admin.sh` refuses to invent
  an account; promotion needs the exact existing email.
- **No alerting.** Health metrics exist but nothing pages an operator when a
  callback fails, income goes unbooked, an amount mismatches, or a request ages
  out.
- **`support@daftari.app` is unverified** as a deliverable, monitored mailbox.
- **Legal text is unreviewed** and still English-only while the interface is
  Kiswahili by default.



Found by on-device verification of v6.14.1. The caching fix was correct, but it
only helps once the **new** service worker takes control. A device that already had
the buggy worker installed still received one stale document, requested a
code-split chunk that the deploy had already purged, and landed on the error screen
instead of their business. One reload fixed it, because by then the new worker had
activated.

Every current tester and beta user has that old worker, so every one of them would
have seen this error once. Not acceptable before inviting anyone to launch.

### Fixed

`ErrorBoundary` now recognises a failed dynamic import and reloads once from the
network. A chunk that cannot be fetched means the document is stale by definition,
and the current document names chunks that do exist.

- Fires only for the three stale-chunk messages browsers actually emit, so an
  ordinary crash is never masked by a reload.
- Guarded by `sessionStorage`, so a genuinely broken build cannot become an
  infinite reload loop on a metered connection.
- The user still sees the recovery screen if the reload does not fix it.

Also widened `captureError`'s context type so the boundary can report
`componentStack` instead of discarding it to satisfy the type checker.

### Tests

6 tests. The one that matters most asserts a **second** failure in the same session
does not reload again: an unconditional reload would be worse than the error screen
it replaces, and would cost the vendor money on a metered connection.

