## [6.14.1] — 2026-09-26 — Fix: returning PWA users got a blank white screen after a deploy

Found by on-device verification immediately after the v6.14.0 deploy. The phone
rendered **nothing** — `#root` was empty — and it was not a flake. This would have
hit **every returning user on every deploy**, which for a first-100 launch means
every existing customer opening the app to a blank page.

It is not a v6.14.0 regression. The defect had been live since the PWA was first
installed; the deploy just exposed it.

### The chain

1. Workbox precached `index.html` and served **every navigation** from that cached
   document: `NavigationRoute(createHandlerBoundToURL('index.html'))`.
2. That document names content-hashed assets. A deploy purges the old ones.
3. Vercel's catch-all SPA rewrite answered the now-missing asset with `index.html`
   and **HTTP 200**.
4. The browser executed HTML as an ES module, the module graph failed, React never
   mounted, and `#root` stayed empty.

Step 3 is what made it silent. A missing asset should be a 404 the app can react
to. Instead it arrived as a 200 with `Content-Type: text/html`, which failed as a
*parse* error with nothing pointing at the cause.

Every unit test was green the whole time. Each layer was individually plausible;
the defect lived in the interaction, which is exactly the class of bug that only
real-device verification finds.

### Fixed

- `navigateFallback: null` and `html` removed from `globPatterns`, so no document is
  ever served from cache. Hashed assets stay precached, which is safe because their
  names are immutable — offline support is not sacrificed.
- Navigations are now `NetworkFirst` with a cache fallback, so a new deploy is
  picked up on the next open and offline still works.
- `vercel.json` excludes `/assets/*` from the SPA rewrite, so a purged asset is a
  real 404 instead of HTML masquerading as a module.
- The generated `registerSW.js` was a bare `register('/sw.js')` with no
  `updateViaCache` and no `controllerchange` handler, so a new worker could
  activate while the page stayed blank and only a **manual refresh** recovered.
  Replaced with a registration that revalidates the worker and reloads on
  `controllerchange`, guarded against reload loops.
- Added a blank-screen recovery net: if `#root` is still empty 4s after load, the
  app reloads once from the network. Guarded by `sessionStorage` so a genuinely
  broken build cannot become an infinite reload loop on a metered connection. This
  is the safety net for the whole bug class, not just this instance.

### Tests

`src/lib/pwaDeploySafety.test.ts` (13 tests) asserts the config that produced the
defect, and every one was verified to fail against the old configuration: reverting
all three files fails 5 of the assertions, and restoring passes all 13.

### A second defect this exposed

`injectRegister: null` stops `vite-plugin-pwa` injecting its `<script>` tag, and I
did not add the reference by hand — so the registration script was never loaded and
the service worker silently stopped registering. Nothing failed in dev: there is no
service worker and no error, which looks identical to a working offline-less page.

CI caught it via `e2e/pwa-prod.spec.ts` ("service worker should register in the
production build"), a suite that exists in this repo and that the local gate list
had never run. Two lessons, both now acted on: `index.html` links `/registerSW.js`
explicitly, and a unit test asserts that link so it fails fast and locally rather
than in CI.

---

## [6.14.0] — 2026-09-26 — Reconciliation UI, legal pages, and a health view that stops lying

v6.13.0 shipped an `stk_health` view and then discovered, by querying production as a
normal client, that the view reported a **false all-clear**. This release fixes that
and builds the product surface on top of it: a customer-facing reconciliation screen,
the legal/privacy pages with a real support contact, and the live M-Pesa numbers.

### Fixed — `stk_health` could not report a callback failure to its owner

`daftari_stk_callbacks` had RLS enabled with **no policies and no `user_id` column**.
A signed-in user could therefore never see a single callback row, which made
`income_write_failed` and `ignored_callbacks` structurally incapable of being
non-zero. Observed in production:

```
service_role sees:      ignored_callbacks = 3
a normal client sees:   ignored_callbacks = 0
```

A reconciliation UI built on that view would have shown a green tick while real
payments were being refused. A monitor that cannot fail is worse than no monitor.

- `20260926000000_stk_callbacks_user_scope.sql` adds `user_id` (backfilled from the
  request each callback resolves) and an owner SELECT policy. Client roles still hold
  no INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE on the table, so an audit row can never be forged.
- The policy deliberately derives visibility from the **checkout** as well as the
  column, so a callback inserted without an owner is still visible to the customer who
  paid. A naive `user_id = auth.uid()` would hide a genuine failure permanently.
- A callback for an unknown checkout matches neither branch, so **forgeries stay
  invisible to every user** — verified against a real Postgres.
- The callback now writes `user_id` on the audit row; 2 new Deno tests cover the owned
  and unowned cases.

### Added

- **Reconciliation screen** (`Settings -> Payment reconciliation`) — per-user payment
  health plus the callback trail. The rule that shapes it: a failed check renders an
  explicit "could not check" error and **never** zeros, because zeros on a failed fetch
  are indistinguishable from a healthy business.
- **Legal screen** (`Settings -> Privacy policy`) — privacy policy, terms of service,
  and a real support contact, with the M-Pesa numbers in one place. Discloses M-Pesa as
  a payment processor and states data-subject rights, which is what the policy is for.
- **`SUPPORT` constant** as the single source of truth for the contact, so the legal
  pages, the upgrade sheet, and reconciliation cannot disagree.
- **Live M-Pesa numbers.** `MPESA_PAYMENT_NUMBER` is now the real receiving line
  (`254703939236`), which unblocks self-serve upgrade collection. `MPESA_ALERT_NUMBER`
  is the separate notification sim (`254798655485`) and is deliberately a different
  value: M-Pesa alerts fire to whichever number the payer dialled from, so sharing the
  receiving line would alert the customer instead of the owner.

### Tests

623 Vitest tests across 59 files (was 586/56) and 10 Deno tests (was 8). The 3 new
files cover the fail-closed health fetch, the screen's error-not-zeros behaviour, the
legal content, and the owned/unowned callback audit rows. All gates green.

### Refused

An operator request to hide the Daraja consumer secret inside the build (an
"invisible" path in the bundle or shell profile) was declined and the reason recorded.
A Vite PWA is static JavaScript delivered to every phone: it is minified, not
encrypted, and anything placed in it is readable by anyone who opens DevTools. Leaking
the consumer secret would let an attacker authenticate to Safaricom as Daftari. The
secrets belong in Supabase's encrypted store, where they already are — the browser
never talks to Daraja, only the edge function does, so there is nothing to hide.

---

## [6.13.0] — 2026-09-25 — Two production-breaking defects found by deploying, not testing

The v6.12.0 suite was fully green (586 tests, 8 Deno tests) and the money path was
still broken in production. Both defects live at the boundary between our code and
the platform, so no local test could see them. This release fixes them and adds the
reconciliation tooling needed to notice the next one.

### Fixed — the callback could never have confirmed a payment

- **`daftari_stk_requests` had no primary key** while being published to
  `supabase_realtime`. PostgreSQL refuses `UPDATE` on a published table with no
  replica identity, so the callback's mark-PAID step failed on every payment: the
  customer was charged, income was written, and the request stayed `PENDING` forever.
  Fixed by `20260925000002_stk_requests_primary_key.sql`, which adds a surrogate
  `id` primary key (idempotent, backfills existing rows, and raises if the PK is
  still missing so this can never fail silently again). It also sets
  `REPLICA IDENTITY FULL` **before** the backfill, because the backfill `UPDATE` is
  itself blocked until a replica identity exists.
- **The callback was deployed with `verify_jwt` on** (the Supabase default).
  Safaricom does not send a Supabase JWT, so every callback was rejected at the
  gateway with `401 UNAUTHORIZED_NO_AUTH_HEADER` before reaching the handler. The
  runbook now marks `--no-verify-jwt` as mandatory for `lipa-na-mpesa-callback` and
  documents the reconciliation checks that replace gateway auth.
- **`20260925000000` now grants `service_role` explicitly** instead of relying on
  Supabase's default grants. `BYPASSRLS` bypasses row-level policies but *not*
  table-level `GRANT`/`REVOKE`, so a revoke without a matching grant would have
  broken the money path in the one direction that must never break. Verified
  against a real Postgres: client writes denied, edge writes allowed.

### Added

- **`stk_health` view** (`20260925000001_stk_health_view.sql`) — one query that
  answers "is the money path healthy?" Every column is a should-be-zero count for
  a *named* failure mode, including `paid_without_income` (money with no books).
  Replaces four ad-hoc reconciliation queries with one, and is the interim answer
  to the known "no reconciliation UI" gap.
- **Structured JSON logging** in both edge functions via
  `_shared/daraja/logging.ts`. Deliberately **not** an exception tracker: the
  callback returns `200` for every outcome by design, so a tracker would report a
  healthy service while money quietly failed. Every refusal, duplicate, and
  income-write failure now emits a greppable line, with phone numbers redacted.
- **Credentials hygiene.** `DARAJA_CLIENT_KEY` / `DARAJA_CLIENT_SECRET` are stored
  only as Supabase secrets, never in a tracked file. `supabase secrets list`
  confirms `DARAJA_PASSKEY`/`SHORTCODE`/`CALLBACK_URL` remain unset, so the
  function fails closed with `503 DARAJA_NOT_CONFIGURED` rather than half-working.

### Tests

- 2 new Deno tests assert the callback **emits a log line** when an income write
  fails and when a payment is confirmed. The log is the only signal that money did
  not post, so an untested log claim would be exactly the false confidence this
  release is correcting.
- Suite: 586 Vitest tests across 56 files, 8 Deno tests, all gates green.

### Corrected

- `docs/LAUNCH-READINESS.md` no longer lists a "trial-expiry job" risk. Expiry is
  enforced at read time on both the server (the `user_subscription` view computes
  `is_active` per query) and the client (`isSubscriptionActive` compares
  `trial_ends_at` to `now`). A cron job would only be row hygiene; no test may
  assume one has run.

---

## [6.12.0] — 2026-09-25 — M-PESA money-path hardening + launch integrity

A launch-readiness audit of v6.11.0 found defects that could make the feature **report a
successful payment as failed**, and **record a payment as settled before the money was
booked**. This release closes them. It changes product behaviour, so it is a minor bump.

### Fixed — money path

- **Payments could be reported as failed when they succeeded.** `StkPushPanel` resolved
  against the Dexie `stk_requests` mirror, which only advances when a sync runs. A payment
  Safaricom had confirmed could sit unread and then be declared expired. Resolution now
  reads the server (`repository.fetchStkRequestFromServer`); the mirror is an offline
  fallback that can prove PAID but never failure. A final server read happens before
  expiry, and a genuinely unknown outcome is reported as **unconfirmed**, not failed.
- **Money was booked after the request was marked PAID, with both errors ignored.** The
  callback now writes the income row first and marks PAID only on success. A duplicate
  callback (`23505`) is treated as already-credited; any other income-write failure leaves
  the request PENDING and records `INCOME_WRITE_FAILED` so an operator can see it. A crash
  between the two steps can no longer produce money-without-books.
- **Retries could charge twice.** `stk-push` refuses a second push while the same
  `local_id` is PENDING (`409 DUPLICATE_PUSH`), and the panel reuses one `local_id` across
  retries.
- **The same sale could be recorded twice.** Manual Save is disabled while a push is in
  flight, closing the path where the callback and the vendor both wrote the sale.
- **The callback had no tests at all** — it was a top-level `serve()` and the only code
  that writes M-PESA income. Extracted to `handler.ts` behind an injectable store, with 7
  Deno tests covering money ordering, duplicate callbacks, income-write failure, forged
  callbacks, and amount mismatch.

### Fixed — fail-closed configuration

- **The upgrade sheet showed a placeholder paybill.** `PRICING.MPESA_PAYMENT_NUMBER`
  shipped as the literal `YOUR_MPESA_NUMBER_HERE`, rendered with a copy button and an
  "I have paid" button. It now ships empty; `MPESA_PAYMENT_CONFIGURED` is derived from it
  so the two cannot drift, and the sheet refuses to collect payment until a real number is
  set.
- `stk-push` returns `LEDGER_UNAVAILABLE` instead of `ok` when it cannot record the
  authoritative PENDING row, and validates `paymentMethod` at the boundary.
- Raw upstream descriptions are no longer rendered to users; unmapped codes show a generic
  failure and log the detail.

### Fixed — data durability

- **The sync queue deadlocked past 50 items.** `flushQueue()` returned `{synced: 0}` and
  opened the circuit breaker, so the count could never fall and writes were stranded on
  the device permanently. `MAX_BATCH` now bounds work per pass and the flush loops until
  the queue drains.
- **Writes were not pushed out.** The queue only flushed on mount and reconnect, so losing
  the device mid-shift lost every sale of that shift. `addToQueue()` now schedules a
  debounced write-through flush (3s quiet period, 15s ceiling) and app background/hide
  forces a final flush.
- **Restore lied about partial failure.** One failing table threw out of
  `pullFromSupabase`, silently skipping every table after it, and customers/daily_closes
  discarded their errors. Each table is now pulled independently and failures are named;
  tables that are not deployed are reported as a separate `skipped` list.

### Fixed — entitlement and schema truth

- `useSubscription` fetched once on mount, so a vendor whose upgrade an operator had just
  approved kept seeing the paywall until they force-closed the app. It now re-checks every
  60s and on foreground. Fail-closed defaults are unchanged.
- Migration `20260925000000` drops INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE on `daftari_stk_requests`. The
  v6.11.0 migration called the table "server-owned" with a "read-only mirror" while
  granting clients full write — reachable from the browser console to flip a PENDING row
  to PAID and forge an `mpesa_code`.

### Added

- `docs/LAUNCH-READINESS.md` — what blocks the first 100 users, the sandbox drill
  estimate, the Safaricom sandbox app requirements, and what the M-PESA integration makes
  possible.
- `docs/changelog/sprint-v6-12-0-mpesa-money-path-hardening.md`.
- `npm run test:functions` (`deno test supabase/functions/`), run in CI.
- Recovery of an in-flight push on re-entry, so a vendor who walks away and returns can
  still see the outcome.

### Honesty correction

- The v6.11.0 entry claimed `check:functions` was "wired into npm + CI". It was only in
  npm — edge code was never typechecked by CI. CI now runs `deno check` **and**
  `deno test` over `supabase/functions/`, and the v6.11.0 entry is corrected in place.

### Operator actions

- `supabase db push` (applies `20260925000000`), deploy both Edge Functions, add the 8
  `DARAJA_*` secrets, run the sandbox drill, and set `PRICING.MPESA_PAYMENT_NUMBER`.
  Nothing is live until this branch merges to `main` — CI deploys only from `main`.

